00:06:07.857 --> 00:06:08.690
CHRIS SANSONE: Sure.

00:06:08.690 --> 00:06:12.710
So Spinnaker is a open source,
multi-cloud, continuous

00:06:12.710 --> 00:06:16.940
delivery tool, and it's used
by companies, enterprises,

00:06:16.940 --> 00:06:21.530
for release management, for
deploying container images,

00:06:21.530 --> 00:06:24.890
artifacts, Deb packages, into
production cloud environments.

00:06:24.890 --> 00:06:27.950
And it's used, basically,
for development teams

00:06:27.950 --> 00:06:33.260
to deploy applications
quickly, reliably, and safely.

00:06:33.260 --> 00:06:35.730
FRANSESC CAMPOY: From a
historical point of view,

00:06:35.730 --> 00:06:37.970
when did Spinnaker happen?

00:06:37.970 --> 00:06:40.100
We just launched-- well, we--

00:06:40.100 --> 00:06:43.760
you, just launched the
1.0 version of Spinnaker.

00:06:43.760 --> 00:06:45.350
But tell us a little
bit of what are

00:06:45.350 --> 00:06:47.434
the origins of this project?

00:06:47.434 --> 00:06:49.100
STEVEN KIM: Yeah,
yeah, Spinnaker, we're

00:06:49.100 --> 00:06:51.950
announcing 1.0 just
now, that really

00:06:51.950 --> 00:06:53.930
follows in the wake
of a tremendous amount

00:06:53.930 --> 00:06:56.267
of concerted effort
that multiple teams

00:06:56.267 --> 00:06:58.100
have put together to
arrive at where we are.

00:06:58.100 --> 00:07:01.910
So Spinnaker, as a project,
started inside Netflix

00:07:01.910 --> 00:07:03.690
about three years ago.

00:07:03.690 --> 00:07:05.370
And it's the way
that they wanted

00:07:05.370 --> 00:07:08.000
to do their
deployments internally.

00:07:08.000 --> 00:07:12.440
And then, about 2 1/2 years
ago or so, Google joined in.

00:07:12.440 --> 00:07:16.210
And this is prior to Spinnaker
being known externally at all.

00:07:16.210 --> 00:07:19.340
It's been sort of a private
project inside Netflix.

00:07:19.340 --> 00:07:24.140
And then, in November of 2015,
we announced open sourcing

00:07:24.140 --> 00:07:25.060
to the public.

00:07:25.060 --> 00:07:26.960
There was a lot of good
coverage around that,

00:07:26.960 --> 00:07:29.480
and since then we've had
a number of organizations

00:07:29.480 --> 00:07:33.020
come on and adopt it for
their own deployment purposes.

00:07:33.020 --> 00:07:35.900
But we knew that there
were a lot of things

00:07:35.900 --> 00:07:38.120
that needed to be done
still to really make

00:07:38.120 --> 00:07:42.080
it ready for mass adoption and
people who didn't want to have

00:07:42.080 --> 00:07:43.580
to tinker with it
too much for it

00:07:43.580 --> 00:07:46.256
to be successful in their
respective organizations.

00:07:46.256 --> 00:07:47.630
So that's what
we've been working

00:07:47.630 --> 00:07:49.710
toward for the last
year and a half or so.

00:07:49.710 --> 00:07:52.670
And that's what the 1.0
is a culmination of.

00:07:52.670 --> 00:07:54.500
MARK MANDEL: So that's
really interesting.

00:07:54.500 --> 00:07:57.230
I'm curious though, what were
the problems that Spinnaker

00:07:57.230 --> 00:08:00.260
was built to solve that weren't
currently covered by existing

00:08:00.260 --> 00:08:01.400
solutions in this space?

00:08:01.400 --> 00:08:03.691
Like, were there particular
things that were a problem?

00:08:03.691 --> 00:08:05.850
Or is there a particular
aim for this project?

00:08:05.850 --> 00:08:08.120
STEVEN KIM: Yeah, I mean,
Spinnaker, inside Netflix,

00:08:08.120 --> 00:08:10.453
for those of you who are
familiar with the project named

00:08:10.453 --> 00:08:13.550
Asgard, which is also developed
internally at Netflix,

00:08:13.550 --> 00:08:17.150
Spinnaker is sort of a
spiritual successor to Asgard.

00:08:17.150 --> 00:08:20.030
And what Netflix
recognized, internally,

00:08:20.030 --> 00:08:24.290
is that they are sort of the
poster child for early adoption

00:08:24.290 --> 00:08:26.750
of public cloud with the AWS.

00:08:26.750 --> 00:08:29.780
And back then, it was
really bare infrastructure

00:08:29.780 --> 00:08:32.240
as a service, and there was
very little tootling out there,

00:08:32.240 --> 00:08:35.900
so thematically, what Netflix
did, very successfully,

00:08:35.900 --> 00:08:39.049
is build a set of
canonical tools internally.

00:08:39.049 --> 00:08:41.659
And what they effectively did
is it raised the infrastructure

00:08:41.659 --> 00:08:45.890
as a service to a internal
platform as a service of sorts,

00:08:45.890 --> 00:08:46.417
right?

00:08:46.417 --> 00:08:48.500
And so the problems that
they were trying to solve

00:08:48.500 --> 00:08:51.850
is to create a canonical
way across Netflix

00:08:51.850 --> 00:08:56.420
by which deployments are made
out to their public cloud.

00:08:56.420 --> 00:08:59.630
And what that means is
taking a specific set

00:08:59.630 --> 00:09:02.360
of opinionated approaches,
best practices,

00:09:02.360 --> 00:09:05.609
lessons learned, about how
deployments should be done.

00:09:05.609 --> 00:09:07.400
And that's something
that we can talk about

00:09:07.400 --> 00:09:08.987
throughout this conversation.

00:09:08.987 --> 00:09:11.570
FRANSESC CAMPOY: Does that mean
that Spinnaker is specifically

00:09:11.570 --> 00:09:13.760
done for [INAUDIBLE]
service architecture,

00:09:13.760 --> 00:09:18.770
or can I use it if I'm just
deploying to virtual machines?

00:09:18.770 --> 00:09:20.750
STEVEN KIM: Yeah,
Spinnaker certainly

00:09:20.750 --> 00:09:24.730
has things that it is first
and foremost built on top of,

00:09:24.730 --> 00:09:28.540
but throughout the
usage across time,

00:09:28.540 --> 00:09:31.560
we have taken on
additional use cases.

00:09:31.560 --> 00:09:36.420
And so today, Spinnaker supports
both VM based deployments

00:09:36.420 --> 00:09:38.420
as well as container based
deployments, and then

00:09:38.420 --> 00:09:41.130
actually also things like App
Engine and Open Stack, as well.

00:09:41.130 --> 00:09:43.860
And although you could say
that it was built on a Micro

00:09:43.860 --> 00:09:46.417
services premise,
it certainly goes

00:09:46.417 --> 00:09:48.125
and works with a number
of different ways

00:09:48.125 --> 00:09:50.600
that you can go ahead and
choose to use Spinnaker.

00:09:50.600 --> 00:09:54.260
So Spinnaker has, certainly,
a set of opinions.

00:09:54.260 --> 00:09:56.120
And it's very
prescriptive about how

00:09:56.120 --> 00:09:58.760
to do certain things, like
immutable infrastructure,

00:09:58.760 --> 00:10:02.060
and blue-green
deployments, and so forth.

00:10:02.060 --> 00:10:03.980
But that doesn't stop
you from using it

00:10:03.980 --> 00:10:06.320
how you want to go ahead
and use it and decide

00:10:06.320 --> 00:10:09.004
for yourself what is the right
increment across time to do so.

00:10:09.004 --> 00:10:09.920
FRANSESC CAMPOY: Nice.

00:10:09.920 --> 00:10:13.340
So this is a tool
that Netflix created

00:10:13.340 --> 00:10:16.070
to help themselves
making their deployments

00:10:16.070 --> 00:10:20.090
more automated while
running on AWS.

00:10:20.090 --> 00:10:21.572
Why is Google involved?

00:10:21.572 --> 00:10:23.030
STEVEN KIM: That's
a good question.

00:10:23.030 --> 00:10:26.990
Google recognized that
for our GCP users,

00:10:26.990 --> 00:10:29.870
we wanted to go ahead and
provide a robust tool set that

00:10:29.870 --> 00:10:32.720
follows in lines with
a lot of opinions

00:10:32.720 --> 00:10:35.000
that Google actually
has internally

00:10:35.000 --> 00:10:36.560
on how we do deployments.

00:10:36.560 --> 00:10:38.600
So that's something
that I think it's

00:10:38.600 --> 00:10:40.430
fair to say we have
a lot of practice at.

00:10:40.430 --> 00:10:42.305
A lot of mistakes were
made, a lot of lessons

00:10:42.305 --> 00:10:44.794
learned, and we have
a set of opinions

00:10:44.794 --> 00:10:47.210
that we follow on how software
deployments should be made.

00:10:47.210 --> 00:10:51.350
That's how Netflix does
4,000 deployments a day,

00:10:51.350 --> 00:10:54.482
that's how Google does
25,000 releases a day,

00:10:54.482 --> 00:10:55.940
you don't just do
that by accident.

00:10:55.940 --> 00:10:57.642
It's through a lot
of lessons learned.

00:10:57.642 --> 00:10:59.600
So we were looking for
a way to go ahead and do

00:10:59.600 --> 00:11:03.050
this externally, and when we
ran into the Spinnaker project

00:11:03.050 --> 00:11:07.100
that Netflix was doing,
we found a kindred spirit.

00:11:07.100 --> 00:11:09.290
You know, on similar
types of problems,

00:11:09.290 --> 00:11:13.740
similar types of scale,
and similar priorities.

00:11:13.740 --> 00:11:14.930
So we jumped in.

00:11:14.930 --> 00:11:19.250
And at that time, Netflix
was deploying almost entirely

00:11:19.250 --> 00:11:23.840
to VMs, so AWSEC2s,
so we started

00:11:23.840 --> 00:11:27.890
without adding GCE VM
support with the intention

00:11:27.890 --> 00:11:29.450
that on an ongoing
basis, we would

00:11:29.450 --> 00:11:34.610
continue to pour in our best
practices for our GCP users.

00:11:34.610 --> 00:11:37.820
And a simple example of that
is we very quickly followed up

00:11:37.820 --> 00:11:41.300
with support for
Kubernetes, and App Engine,

00:11:41.300 --> 00:11:43.310
and then a whole
bunch of other things

00:11:43.310 --> 00:11:46.017
that were founded based
on things at Google.

00:11:46.017 --> 00:11:48.350
MARK MANDEL: Oh, nice, so you
mentioned, too, there, you

00:11:48.350 --> 00:11:51.350
said Kubernetes, I'm assuming
Container Engine, App Engine.

00:11:51.350 --> 00:11:53.270
Are there any other
internal projects

00:11:53.270 --> 00:11:56.570
that this supports deployment to
or are those just the only two?

00:11:56.570 --> 00:12:02.250
STEVEN KIM: Yeah, the full list
is AWSEC2, GCVMs, App Engine,

00:12:02.250 --> 00:12:04.910
Kubernetes, or
GKE, and that could

00:12:04.910 --> 00:12:10.550
be Kuberntes running on EC2s,
or GCVMs, or bare metal.

00:12:10.550 --> 00:12:14.450
Also supports Microsoft
Azure, OpenStack,

00:12:14.450 --> 00:12:17.090
and there's a couple of others
that are in flight right now

00:12:17.090 --> 00:12:18.950
such as DCOS.

00:12:18.950 --> 00:12:21.050
Mesosphere DCOS is in flight.

00:12:21.050 --> 00:12:24.140
Oracle Cloud bare
metal is also in flight

00:12:24.140 --> 00:12:25.790
right now, that's
pretty much complete.

00:12:25.790 --> 00:12:27.440
That should be coming
out pretty soon.

00:12:27.440 --> 00:12:29.856
And I'm sure we'll have many
more to go ahead and announce

00:12:29.856 --> 00:12:30.910
as time goes forward.

00:12:30.910 --> 00:12:34.160
FRANSESC CAMPOY: Cool, so that
means basically anyone could

00:12:34.160 --> 00:12:38.210
be using Spinnaker, because
no matter what you're running,

00:12:38.210 --> 00:12:39.650
Spinnaker will run for you.

00:12:39.650 --> 00:12:41.390
And it's also open-source.

00:12:41.390 --> 00:12:43.942
I'm going to say that,
I'm assuming, at least.

00:12:43.942 --> 00:12:45.400
STEVEN KIM: Yeah,
it's open source,

00:12:45.400 --> 00:12:47.870
which also means two things
that are important to us.

00:12:47.870 --> 00:12:50.430
One of them is that it's free.

00:12:50.430 --> 00:12:53.420
And number two is that
we welcome full requests.

00:12:53.420 --> 00:12:55.190
And so we're very
much also focused

00:12:55.190 --> 00:12:59.630
on building the right
community at scale

00:12:59.630 --> 00:13:00.669
and then sustainability.

00:13:00.669 --> 00:13:02.210
FRANSESC CAMPOY: So
I have a question

00:13:02.210 --> 00:13:05.160
that I ask every single
time, but in what language

00:13:05.160 --> 00:13:06.872
is this written?

00:13:06.872 --> 00:13:09.372
STEVEN KIM: Hey, Christopher
what language is it written in?

00:13:09.372 --> 00:13:10.330
CHRIS SANSONE: Swahili.

00:13:12.694 --> 00:13:14.360
MARK MANDEL: And that
is the best answer

00:13:14.360 --> 00:13:16.460
anyone has ever given.

00:13:16.460 --> 00:13:20.750
STEVEN KIM: Spinnaker is
written in Java, Groovy,

00:13:20.750 --> 00:13:24.872
and actually a lot of Kotlin is
beginning to make its way in,

00:13:24.872 --> 00:13:27.080
and then of course the front
end, the web components,

00:13:27.080 --> 00:13:30.914
are written by the standard
JS, and CSS, and HTML.

00:13:30.914 --> 00:13:33.330
FRANSESC CAMPOY: Actually, my
question, where it was going

00:13:33.330 --> 00:13:37.610
was anyone can use Spinnaker
because it's open source

00:13:37.610 --> 00:13:41.180
and it's available to deploy
on basically any platform,

00:13:41.180 --> 00:13:43.215
but who should be using it?

00:13:43.215 --> 00:13:45.090
CHRIS SANSONE: Yeah,
that's a great question.

00:13:45.090 --> 00:13:48.440
So I think if you look at
why Spinnaker created it

00:13:48.440 --> 00:13:51.910
and why we've seen companies
like Waze and Target adopt it,

00:13:51.910 --> 00:13:53.540
you know, the
solutions out there

00:13:53.540 --> 00:13:55.880
in the continuous
delivery space are not

00:13:55.880 --> 00:13:58.380
as robust as on the
CI side of things.

00:13:58.380 --> 00:14:01.610
So what we're seeing people
do is stitch together

00:14:01.610 --> 00:14:04.236
a bunch of Jenkins
scripts to recreate,

00:14:04.236 --> 00:14:06.110
essentially, the
functionality that comes out

00:14:06.110 --> 00:14:08.990
of the box with Spinnaker, like
doing a blue-green deployment

00:14:08.990 --> 00:14:10.940
or a rollback.

00:14:10.940 --> 00:14:12.595
And it's pretty
complicated to do that.

00:14:12.595 --> 00:14:14.720
You could do it with Jenkins,
you could do anything

00:14:14.720 --> 00:14:17.690
with Jenkins, but it
wasn't really designed

00:14:17.690 --> 00:14:18.890
for that purpose.

00:14:18.890 --> 00:14:21.220
And so people are having
to build their own tools

00:14:21.220 --> 00:14:24.140
or are coming from a
configuration management

00:14:24.140 --> 00:14:26.960
background, where they're using
things like Chef and Puppet

00:14:26.960 --> 00:14:30.100
to update running
machines versus using

00:14:30.100 --> 00:14:32.240
an immutable infrastructure
to deploy an updated

00:14:32.240 --> 00:14:33.510
fleet of servers.

00:14:33.510 --> 00:14:36.290
So enterprises that
care about uptime,

00:14:36.290 --> 00:14:38.600
care about higher
velocity releases,

00:14:38.600 --> 00:14:40.070
would benefit a
lot from Spinnaker

00:14:40.070 --> 00:14:42.191
without having to recreate
the wheel themselves.

00:14:42.191 --> 00:14:43.940
Because these are
fundamental requirements

00:14:43.940 --> 00:14:46.560
for their business, but not
knowing about Spinnaker,

00:14:46.560 --> 00:14:48.710
a lot of people have
gone to a lot of effort

00:14:48.710 --> 00:14:50.549
to do what Spinnaker
does for you.

00:14:50.549 --> 00:14:52.340
FRANSESC CAMPOY: So
you mentioned something

00:14:52.340 --> 00:14:53.756
that I think is
quite interesting.

00:14:53.756 --> 00:14:57.570
You said this phrase,
blue-green deployment.

00:14:57.570 --> 00:14:59.570
Could you explain a little
bit for our audience,

00:14:59.570 --> 00:15:01.710
what does that mean?

00:15:01.710 --> 00:15:03.920
CHRIS SANSONE: Sure, so
blue-green, or red-black,

00:15:03.920 --> 00:15:05.900
as it's actually called
in the Spinnaker world,

00:15:05.900 --> 00:15:09.530
is a department
where you essentially

00:15:09.530 --> 00:15:12.590
deploy a new version
of your application

00:15:12.590 --> 00:15:15.330
to another running fleet.

00:15:15.330 --> 00:15:17.860
But you keep your old
one around and you just

00:15:17.860 --> 00:15:21.110
simply move your traffic
over from your old service

00:15:21.110 --> 00:15:22.434
to your new service.

00:15:22.434 --> 00:15:24.350
But you don't spin down
the old one quite yet,

00:15:24.350 --> 00:15:27.540
you leave it around in case
there's an issue or a bug

00:15:27.540 --> 00:15:29.450
and need to do a
rollback, then it's simply

00:15:29.450 --> 00:15:31.790
as easy as re-diverting
your traffic back

00:15:31.790 --> 00:15:35.060
to the original versus having to
sort of spin it up or redeploy

00:15:35.060 --> 00:15:35.900
from scratch.

00:15:35.900 --> 00:15:38.540
STEVEN KIM: And also, it's
worth mentioning, blue-green or

00:15:38.540 --> 00:15:41.540
red-black strategy is one
that we can get in point to,

00:15:41.540 --> 00:15:43.590
but obviously, it's not
a one size fits all.

00:15:43.590 --> 00:15:47.660
So some organizations might
say blue-green deployments

00:15:47.660 --> 00:15:50.040
inherently required to
x number of resources

00:15:50.040 --> 00:15:52.789
to go ahead and do a deployment,
that is not tenable for them.

00:15:52.789 --> 00:15:54.080
So we hear that loud and clear.

00:15:54.080 --> 00:15:56.455
We're working on other things
like what we call a rolling

00:15:56.455 --> 00:15:59.900
red-black, for example, which
makes better use of resource

00:15:59.900 --> 00:16:01.275
overhead.

00:16:01.275 --> 00:16:02.900
So if you're interested
in hearing more

00:16:02.900 --> 00:16:04.580
about what that is, you should
definitely check that out

00:16:04.580 --> 00:16:06.470
on the website to
get more details.

00:16:06.470 --> 00:16:08.595
MARK MANDEL: Christopher,
you said something almost

00:16:08.595 --> 00:16:10.610
in passing there, too,
and you said basically

00:16:10.610 --> 00:16:13.400
that Spinnaker seems to be
a tool just for enterprise,

00:16:13.400 --> 00:16:15.740
or you just used the word
enterprise in a sentence.

00:16:15.740 --> 00:16:18.470
I'm wondering, right, if
I'm part of a small team--

00:16:18.470 --> 00:16:20.870
or when should I get started
with Spinnaker is probably

00:16:20.870 --> 00:16:22.670
the best question.

00:16:22.670 --> 00:16:24.503
CHRIS SANSONE: Yeah,
that's a good question.

00:16:24.503 --> 00:16:26.510
So I think if you are
experiencing any pain

00:16:26.510 --> 00:16:29.760
around releasing
your application,

00:16:29.760 --> 00:16:31.320
it might be worth looking at.

00:16:31.320 --> 00:16:33.690
I think that since
Spinnaker is open source,

00:16:33.690 --> 00:16:35.660
you do have to stand
up your own instance,

00:16:35.660 --> 00:16:37.799
it's not a hosted SaaS service.

00:16:37.799 --> 00:16:39.340
We, as part of this
1.0 launch, we're

00:16:39.340 --> 00:16:40.881
launching a new tool
called Halliard,

00:16:40.881 --> 00:16:45.150
which is a CLI command line tool
for installing and configuring

00:16:45.150 --> 00:16:45.650
Spinnaker.

00:16:45.650 --> 00:16:49.020
So, for people that have
tried Spinnaker in the past,

00:16:49.020 --> 00:16:51.500
Halliard greatly
lowers the barrier

00:16:51.500 --> 00:16:53.510
for getting it up
and running yourself.

00:16:53.510 --> 00:16:56.750
So I think if you are a
development team that kind of

00:16:56.750 --> 00:16:59.699
does your own DevOps
using cloud services

00:16:59.699 --> 00:17:01.490
and are comfortable in
that world, that you

00:17:01.490 --> 00:17:02.970
could benefit from Spinnaker.

00:17:02.970 --> 00:17:05.180
You know, I think
what a lot of people

00:17:05.180 --> 00:17:08.240
start with is this post
build script deployment.

00:17:08.240 --> 00:17:10.579
You know, as part of their
CI system, they'll do a fire

00:17:10.579 --> 00:17:12.619
and forget deploy, post bill.

00:17:12.619 --> 00:17:14.660
They'll [INAUDIBLE]
apply, or G Cloud

00:17:14.660 --> 00:17:16.250
app deploy, something like that.

00:17:16.250 --> 00:17:17.602
If that works for you, great.

00:17:17.602 --> 00:17:19.310
But I think at some
point, most companies

00:17:19.310 --> 00:17:22.160
are looking for a more
sophisticated, more stable

00:17:22.160 --> 00:17:23.147
deployment strategy.

00:17:23.147 --> 00:17:24.730
STEVEN KIM: There's
an inherent aspect

00:17:24.730 --> 00:17:26.359
of the question
you're asking which

00:17:26.359 --> 00:17:30.110
is, when does it become worth
the overhead of operating

00:17:30.110 --> 00:17:30.680
Spinnaker?

00:17:30.680 --> 00:17:30.980
Right?

00:17:30.980 --> 00:17:33.355
For your organization to think
about reaping the benefits

00:17:33.355 --> 00:17:34.430
that Spinnaker brings.

00:17:34.430 --> 00:17:37.450
And really, to the limit, we
hope that operationalizing--

00:17:37.450 --> 00:17:38.930
if that's a word--

00:17:38.930 --> 00:17:41.270
Spinnaker approaches a zero.

00:17:41.270 --> 00:17:43.760
Another interesting analogy
to make is, for those of you

00:17:43.760 --> 00:17:45.832
who are familiar
with App Engine,

00:17:45.832 --> 00:17:47.540
startup teams and
small development teams

00:17:47.540 --> 00:17:49.130
loved App Engine
because it was very

00:17:49.130 --> 00:17:52.580
easy to go ahead and deploy
code, and all they needed to do

00:17:52.580 --> 00:17:54.900
was concern themselves
with developer concerns,

00:17:54.900 --> 00:17:58.130
which is writing features, and
then App Engine, as a platform,

00:17:58.130 --> 00:17:59.990
would go ahead and
deploy that for you.

00:17:59.990 --> 00:18:02.390
And if you look at App
Engine, it's a runtime,

00:18:02.390 --> 00:18:05.390
but it's also a set
of front end services

00:18:05.390 --> 00:18:08.000
that allow you to go and
deploy code, and manage

00:18:08.000 --> 00:18:09.830
load balancers, and
traffic, and so forth.

00:18:09.830 --> 00:18:11.960
That's what was really
neat about App Engine.

00:18:11.960 --> 00:18:15.600
So if you imagine, as an analog,
Kubernetes is a platform.

00:18:15.600 --> 00:18:18.170
It's an excellent, and
wonderful runtime platform.

00:18:18.170 --> 00:18:20.540
And then, right now,
we are doing a lot

00:18:20.540 --> 00:18:22.470
as a community, the
Kubernetes community,

00:18:22.470 --> 00:18:25.100
trying to go ahead and make
the tooling in front of it

00:18:25.100 --> 00:18:25.770
much easier.

00:18:25.770 --> 00:18:28.790
So imagine if Spinnaker
were that front end tooling.

00:18:28.790 --> 00:18:30.620
Where, to the limit
that setting up

00:18:30.620 --> 00:18:33.320
Spinnaker becomes
a no ops operation,

00:18:33.320 --> 00:18:36.410
then you would prefer to go
ahead and do your deployments.

00:18:36.410 --> 00:18:39.090
And Kubernetes starts to look
a lot more like App Engine,

00:18:39.090 --> 00:18:41.381
or, for example, if you simply
go ahead and push code 2

00:18:41.381 --> 00:18:43.220
repository, out of the
box, without writing

00:18:43.220 --> 00:18:46.520
your own Jenkins scripts,
or whatever it might be,

00:18:46.520 --> 00:18:49.305
that it comes out of the box
configured to take that code,

00:18:49.305 --> 00:18:50.805
build a container,
and then go ahead

00:18:50.805 --> 00:18:51.680
and deploy it out
to the [INAUDIBLE]

00:18:51.680 --> 00:18:53.030
running Kubernetes cluster.

00:18:53.030 --> 00:18:54.080
MARK MANDEL: So
you actually almost

00:18:54.080 --> 00:18:55.163
raised a good point there.

00:18:55.163 --> 00:18:56.729
So if I'm using App Engine or--

00:18:56.729 --> 00:18:58.520
I mean, the deployment
tools for Kubernetes

00:18:58.520 --> 00:18:59.520
are actually quite nice.

00:18:59.520 --> 00:19:02.670
You can do rolling updates,
red-green's not so hard--

00:19:02.670 --> 00:19:04.870
why then do I want to put
Spinnaker in front of it?

00:19:04.870 --> 00:19:06.450
What part is that
in that story that

00:19:06.450 --> 00:19:07.617
makes this very compelling?

00:19:07.617 --> 00:19:09.950
STEVEN KIM: Yeah, I mean, App
Engine definitely provides

00:19:09.950 --> 00:19:12.170
a lot of automic operations
to go ahead and do things

00:19:12.170 --> 00:19:14.660
like cut over traffic,
A/B testing, and so forth.

00:19:14.660 --> 00:19:17.772
But it's a little more difficult
in a low level for you to go

00:19:17.772 --> 00:19:18.980
ahead and script that, right?

00:19:18.980 --> 00:19:20.960
So you need to make G Cloud
calls to go ahead and make

00:19:20.960 --> 00:19:21.740
those updates.

00:19:21.740 --> 00:19:23.420
With Spinnaker,
we have pipelines

00:19:23.420 --> 00:19:27.400
by which can then create
a repeatable and, really,

00:19:27.400 --> 00:19:30.454
a workflow for yourself
that then grows with you,

00:19:30.454 --> 00:19:32.120
and matures over time,
that you go ahead

00:19:32.120 --> 00:19:33.911
and get feedback from,
and so forth, right?

00:19:33.911 --> 00:19:35.910
It's a benefit of
automating these things.

00:19:35.910 --> 00:19:37.380
CHRIS SANSONE: Yeah, and this
is a really important point.

00:19:37.380 --> 00:19:39.350
So this way you
use Spinnaker, is

00:19:39.350 --> 00:19:41.550
that you create
pipelines deploying

00:19:41.550 --> 00:19:43.700
to different environments
that you specify.

00:19:43.700 --> 00:19:46.400
And, as part of that
pipeline, you defined stages.

00:19:46.400 --> 00:19:48.620
And so those stages can be
a lot of different things.

00:19:48.620 --> 00:19:50.360
They can be a
manual approval, it

00:19:50.360 --> 00:19:53.000
could be a container
job, a Jenkins script.

00:19:53.000 --> 00:19:55.670
So it's not simply
the act of deploying,

00:19:55.670 --> 00:19:57.920
but it's the verification
and validation

00:19:57.920 --> 00:19:59.900
that you do as part
of your deployment

00:19:59.900 --> 00:20:02.150
or even prior to
saying that, yes, this

00:20:02.150 --> 00:20:03.990
is production ready code.

00:20:03.990 --> 00:20:07.300
So running load tests,
things like that

00:20:07.300 --> 00:20:11.140
that simply focus in on
just the deployment piece

00:20:11.140 --> 00:20:12.240
doesn't get you.

00:20:12.240 --> 00:20:14.073
STEVEN KIM: And you
know, I'll take the part

00:20:14.073 --> 00:20:16.300
to emphasize the other
side of that coin, which

00:20:16.300 --> 00:20:19.200
is the infrastructure
mutating operations

00:20:19.200 --> 00:20:21.520
that Spinnaker supports
our very, very special.

00:20:21.520 --> 00:20:22.650
And this is a significance.

00:20:22.650 --> 00:20:24.490
So, for example, if
you start with Jenkins

00:20:24.490 --> 00:20:26.500
today, you're
typically interacting

00:20:26.500 --> 00:20:29.260
with your Cloud platform via
embedded G Cloud commands

00:20:29.260 --> 00:20:32.170
inside your Jenkins jobs and
stages and so forth, right?

00:20:32.170 --> 00:20:35.470
Well, Spinnaker comes, out of
the box, first class cognisant

00:20:35.470 --> 00:20:38.050
of all the platform and
the respective operations.

00:20:38.050 --> 00:20:39.430
And here's what that means.

00:20:39.430 --> 00:20:42.110
If you want to do-- like our
tagline that we talk about

00:20:42.110 --> 00:20:43.900
is to do safe deployments.

00:20:43.900 --> 00:20:48.100
And what that means is this is
a supervised safe deployment so

00:20:48.100 --> 00:20:51.262
that when something fails in
the numerous sequence of things

00:20:51.262 --> 00:20:52.720
that you have to
do to the platform

00:20:52.720 --> 00:20:54.790
to do things like
blue-green to deployments,

00:20:54.790 --> 00:20:57.190
that Spinnaker comes out
of the box understanding

00:20:57.190 --> 00:20:58.890
the sequence of things that you
need to go ahead and complete,

00:20:58.890 --> 00:21:00.625
and how you need go ahead
and roll those things back.

00:21:00.625 --> 00:21:03.310
And what that means is, out
of the box, a lot of the work

00:21:03.310 --> 00:21:05.020
that you would have
to do to really see

00:21:05.020 --> 00:21:07.750
that full realization is
done for you, out of the box.

00:21:07.750 --> 00:21:11.170
And that's different for
GCVMs, versus the Kubernetes

00:21:11.170 --> 00:21:14.119
API, versus the Azure
API, for example, right?

00:21:14.119 --> 00:21:16.660
And that's what the benefit of
supporting multiple clouds out

00:21:16.660 --> 00:21:18.229
of the box actually means.

00:21:18.229 --> 00:21:19.270
FRANSESC CAMPOY: Awesome.

00:21:19.270 --> 00:21:21.610
You started talking about steps.

00:21:21.610 --> 00:21:24.160
It made me think of
a Container Builder.

00:21:24.160 --> 00:21:26.680
So I'm wondering now,
what is the relationship

00:21:26.680 --> 00:21:29.570
between Container
Builder and Spinnaker?

00:21:29.570 --> 00:21:33.640
Are they competitors and you
have a very interesting job?

00:21:33.640 --> 00:21:35.540
Or do they work together?

00:21:35.540 --> 00:21:36.550
How does this work?

00:21:36.550 --> 00:21:39.190
CHRIS SANSONE: They are there
they are best of friends.

00:21:39.190 --> 00:21:42.940
So Container Builder
is really around

00:21:42.940 --> 00:21:44.530
your build and test workflow.

00:21:44.530 --> 00:21:48.720
What we see for containers or if
you're building a Deb package,

00:21:48.720 --> 00:21:51.880
is that Container Builder,
you can set up your triggers

00:21:51.880 --> 00:21:52.810
on source.

00:21:52.810 --> 00:21:54.890
And so you make a
change to source,

00:21:54.890 --> 00:21:56.640
you build and test
your release candidate,

00:21:56.640 --> 00:21:59.440
you put it somewhere, or
even do a Docker registry,

00:21:59.440 --> 00:22:02.380
or artifactory, or something
like that, or GCS, S3,

00:22:02.380 --> 00:22:03.200
what have you.

00:22:03.200 --> 00:22:05.590
And then that new
release candidate

00:22:05.590 --> 00:22:08.950
kicks off your Spinnaker
release pipeline.

00:22:08.950 --> 00:22:12.160
And so, you know, as
you say, build steps,

00:22:12.160 --> 00:22:14.980
you have a certain number of
processes or transformations

00:22:14.980 --> 00:22:16.450
that you want to make
against your source code

00:22:16.450 --> 00:22:17.575
as you build your artifact.

00:22:17.575 --> 00:22:19.810
So running unit tests,
integration tests,

00:22:19.810 --> 00:22:21.020
things like that.

00:22:21.020 --> 00:22:23.530
And then there's a
different set of stages

00:22:23.530 --> 00:22:26.500
that you want your now release
candidate to pass through

00:22:26.500 --> 00:22:27.850
as you deploy it.

00:22:27.850 --> 00:22:30.560
And so they're really two
sides of the same coin.

00:22:30.560 --> 00:22:33.406
And it's kind of
the CI and CD sides.

00:22:33.406 --> 00:22:34.780
It gets a little
more complicated

00:22:34.780 --> 00:22:38.380
than that in the sense that you
can trigger Spinnaker pipelines

00:22:38.380 --> 00:22:40.270
from Git events,
and you can actually

00:22:40.270 --> 00:22:42.730
do a CLI based
deployment from Container

00:22:42.730 --> 00:22:44.170
Builder as a build step.

00:22:44.170 --> 00:22:46.540
I will leave that to
power users to find out,

00:22:46.540 --> 00:22:49.720
but the simple
story is one of time

00:22:49.720 --> 00:22:52.120
to get a Container Builder
plus a registry to Spinnaker.

00:22:52.120 --> 00:22:52.870
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:22:52.870 --> 00:22:54.835
So, just so it's
100% clear, a user

00:22:54.835 --> 00:22:56.710
would sit down and be
like, all right, so I'm

00:22:56.710 --> 00:22:58.300
going to do the
continuous integration,

00:22:58.300 --> 00:23:00.020
I'm going to use a product
like Container Builder,

00:23:00.020 --> 00:23:01.728
or maybe Jenkins, or
Travis, or whatever,

00:23:01.728 --> 00:23:03.550
to do my integration,
my tests, make my

00:23:03.550 --> 00:23:05.170
build artifacts
for when I'm done.

00:23:05.170 --> 00:23:07.780
And then I'm going to take
Spinnaker to actually take

00:23:07.780 --> 00:23:10.090
those artifacts and deploy
them to the cloud in a very

00:23:10.090 --> 00:23:10.600
safe manner.

00:23:10.600 --> 00:23:11.170
Is that pretty accurate?

00:23:11.170 --> 00:23:13.336
CHRIS SANSONE: Yeah, that's
a very typical workflow.

00:23:13.336 --> 00:23:15.040
Spinnaker has first
class integrations

00:23:15.040 --> 00:23:17.560
with Travis CI, and Jenkins,
so you can kick off pipelines

00:23:17.560 --> 00:23:19.780
from those CI systems directly.

00:23:19.780 --> 00:23:21.369
And yeah, if that's
your entry point,

00:23:21.369 --> 00:23:23.410
you know you have this
thing being built already,

00:23:23.410 --> 00:23:25.030
and now I want to deploy it.

00:23:25.030 --> 00:23:25.946
FRANSESC CAMPOY: Cool.

