00:24:34.860 --> 00:24:35.590
CHRIS SANSONE: No,
but absolutely.

00:24:35.590 --> 00:24:38.050
There's actually a lot of really
exciting things coming out

00:24:38.050 --> 00:24:38.633
the door soon.

00:24:38.633 --> 00:24:40.720
So one of the big
requests we get

00:24:40.720 --> 00:24:43.550
is for configuration
driven pipeline management.

00:24:43.550 --> 00:24:45.130
So we're working
on something called

00:24:45.130 --> 00:24:48.190
declarative continuous delivery,
where Spinnaker is primarily

00:24:48.190 --> 00:24:50.920
UI driven, although there is
an API in JSON for managing it

00:24:50.920 --> 00:24:54.940
that way, but we're simplifying
config driven pipeline

00:24:54.940 --> 00:24:56.920
templates for
sharing between teams

00:24:56.920 --> 00:24:58.840
and getting set up easier.

00:24:58.840 --> 00:25:01.690
Another really interesting one
is automated canary analysis.

00:25:01.690 --> 00:25:05.410
So we talked about rolling
red-black deployments

00:25:05.410 --> 00:25:07.660
and traffic splitting,
and this then

00:25:07.660 --> 00:25:10.770
integrates with monitoring
services, so Stackdriver,

00:25:10.770 --> 00:25:14.850
Datadog, and Prometheus,
to evaluate your deployment

00:25:14.850 --> 00:25:17.760
and then make automated
judgments based on how it's

00:25:17.760 --> 00:25:20.670
looking around error
rates and queue latenecy

00:25:20.670 --> 00:25:23.040
and automatically
continuing with your release

00:25:23.040 --> 00:25:25.500
or rolling it back for
you, which is pretty cool.

00:25:25.500 --> 00:25:26.430
MARK MANDEL: Awesome.

