00:26:44.822 --> 00:26:47.280
We mentioned this last week
about cloud source repositories

00:26:47.280 --> 00:26:48.780
going general availability.

00:26:48.780 --> 00:26:52.520
But the documentation
is a little confusing.

00:26:52.520 --> 00:26:53.812
We won't shy away from that.

00:26:53.812 --> 00:26:55.770
So basically it says,
cloud source repositories

00:26:55.770 --> 00:27:00.070
is free for up to five project
users per billing account.

00:27:00.070 --> 00:27:02.070
And then, when you actually
dig into the pricing

00:27:02.070 --> 00:27:05.310
on that, as well, it says, once
you go over five project users,

00:27:05.310 --> 00:27:09.630
it's $1 per project user over
5 project users per month.

00:27:09.630 --> 00:27:12.270
I was like, what
is a project user?

00:27:12.270 --> 00:27:14.940
FRANSESC CAMPOY: Yeah, it is
pretty confusing, especially

00:27:14.940 --> 00:27:16.811
because what does
it mean to access?

00:27:16.811 --> 00:27:17.560
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:27:17.560 --> 00:27:19.601
FRANSESC CAMPOY: Does it'
mean that I have access

00:27:19.601 --> 00:27:20.880
or that I do access it, right?

00:27:20.880 --> 00:27:21.630
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:27:21.630 --> 00:27:25.440
FRANSESC CAMPOY: So what we
did, we simply go and ask.

00:27:25.440 --> 00:27:27.840
So we asked our dear
friend Chris Sells,

00:27:27.840 --> 00:27:30.050
he's a product manager here
at Google Cloud Platform,

00:27:30.050 --> 00:27:32.610
and we've had them
on the podcast twice.

00:27:32.610 --> 00:27:35.250
We had him on
episode number five,

00:27:35.250 --> 00:27:38.620
a long time ago, on
developer experience.

00:27:38.620 --> 00:27:42.240
And then again, we had him
for episode 49 with Windows

00:27:42.240 --> 00:27:44.130
and .NET.

00:27:44.130 --> 00:27:48.030
So we asked him, and his
answer was basically saying,

00:27:48.030 --> 00:27:49.830
the important thing
to know is it is not

00:27:49.830 --> 00:27:52.680
about the potential of
accessing a project account,

00:27:52.680 --> 00:27:54.942
it's actually
accessing the project.

00:27:54.942 --> 00:27:57.150
MARK MANDEL: OK, so if I'm
working on something and I

00:27:57.150 --> 00:28:00.150
go and I pull something out of
GitHub, that's me accessing it.

00:28:00.150 --> 00:28:01.566
FRANSESC CAMPOY:
Well, not GitHub.

00:28:01.566 --> 00:28:03.720
MARK MANDEL: Not GitHub,
out of the Git repository

00:28:03.720 --> 00:28:06.010
on the outsource repository.

00:28:06.010 --> 00:28:08.320
FRANSESC CAMPOY: Yeah, and
this is reset per month.

00:28:08.320 --> 00:28:10.920
Which means that, for
instance, let's say that--

00:28:10.920 --> 00:28:12.956
there's two ways I
can see it, right?

00:28:12.956 --> 00:28:14.080
I'm the only person, right?

00:28:14.080 --> 00:28:14.370
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:28:14.370 --> 00:28:17.110
FRANSESC CAMPOY: And I'm working
on four different projects

00:28:17.110 --> 00:28:17.610
this month.

00:28:17.610 --> 00:28:18.210
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:28:18.210 --> 00:28:19.170
FRANSESC CAMPOY: And
then, next month, I'm

00:28:19.170 --> 00:28:21.630
working for completely
different projects.

00:28:21.630 --> 00:28:23.790
That is four projects a
month and four projects

00:28:23.790 --> 00:28:25.350
together, so everything is free.

00:28:25.350 --> 00:28:27.750
It doesn't count as
eight because I accessed

00:28:27.750 --> 00:28:28.890
eight projects in total.

00:28:28.890 --> 00:28:32.880
In the same way, if you have,
let's say, one single project,

00:28:32.880 --> 00:28:34.710
but you have four people, right?

00:28:34.710 --> 00:28:37.680
And you have four people one
month, and then the month

00:28:37.680 --> 00:28:40.180
after that is four
completely different people.

00:28:40.180 --> 00:28:40.620
MARK MANDEL: That's
still four people.

00:28:40.620 --> 00:28:41.730
FRANSESC CAMPOY: That's
still four people.

00:28:41.730 --> 00:28:44.396
And four people is not eight
people or at any point,

00:28:44.396 --> 00:28:46.020
therefore, you do
not pay for anything.

00:28:46.020 --> 00:28:46.420
You're still free.

00:28:46.420 --> 00:28:46.920
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:28:46.920 --> 00:28:48.400
Let's talk a bit about
like a project user,

00:28:48.400 --> 00:28:49.800
as well, just very
quickly, so that that's

00:28:49.800 --> 00:28:50.700
understood, as well.

00:28:50.700 --> 00:28:50.850
FRANSESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:28:50.850 --> 00:28:52.440
MARK MANDEL: So
that's the combination

00:28:52.440 --> 00:28:54.760
of the unique combination
of a project and a user.

00:28:54.760 --> 00:28:57.600
So if I, personally, one
person use one project,

00:28:57.600 --> 00:28:58.710
that's one project user.

00:28:58.710 --> 00:29:00.540
If I go off and
use two projects,

00:29:00.540 --> 00:29:01.910
that's two project users.

00:29:01.910 --> 00:29:04.760
If Francesc and I
both use two projects,

00:29:04.760 --> 00:29:06.180
that's for project users.

00:29:06.180 --> 00:29:08.220
Because it's the unique
combination of those two

00:29:08.220 --> 00:29:08.880
things.

00:29:08.880 --> 00:29:10.171
FRANSESC CAMPOY: Indeed, it is.

00:29:10.171 --> 00:29:13.330
We actually count, did you
actually access it or not?

00:29:13.330 --> 00:29:15.960
It's not about like,
the potential access

00:29:15.960 --> 00:29:18.090
through IAM or
whatever, no, it's did

00:29:18.090 --> 00:29:19.570
you actually access it or not?

00:29:19.570 --> 00:29:21.120
MARK MANDEL: I believe this
stuff is going to get updated,

00:29:21.120 --> 00:29:23.127
so it will become
clearer, but for now.

